        "ggbfikHMutm_text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\">You've probably already tried something.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\"><strong>Distraction techniques.</strong> Stop-and-start methods. A spray or a pill that numbed more than it helped.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\">And it either did nothing or made things worse.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\"><strong>Here's why:</strong></span></p><p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\">Numbing sprays remove sensation without touching the actual cause — you're not solving the control problem, you're just muting it.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\">Mental tricks work around the anxiety without touching what's driving it hormonally.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\">T boosters try to raise testosterone without removing the cortisol ceiling suppressing it. The hormone rises. Cortisol pushes it back down.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\"><strong>&nbsp;The cycle continues.</strong></span></p><p><br>&nbsp;</p><p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\">So you conclude this is just how you are.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\">That conclusion makes sense.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size:14px;\">It's also wrong.</span></p>",
